
What does CallerRunsPolicy do exactly?

If the pool is overloaded, instead of dropping the task, it will:

➡️ Run the task on the calling thread.

So if your thread pool is full, the thread that called your async method will temporarily do the work.

Example:

Your controller calls:

emailService.sendPasswordResetEmail(...);


If the pool is at capacity:

Instead of rejecting the email task

Spring will run the email sending logic inside the controller thread

This means:

The email still gets sent ✔

No task is lost ✔

The system slows slightly to avoid meltdown ✔

You avoid queue explosions and OOM errors ✔

It is a safe fallback mode.
Request 121: caller thread runs email
Request 122: its own caller thread runs email
Request 123: its own caller thread runs email
...
Request 500: its own caller thread runs email
📌 So how many run at the same time?

It depends on:

✔ How many HTTP requests arrive at exactly the same time

(because each one has its own thread)

✔ Your server thread pool size

(usually 200+ in production)

So even if async pool is full:

Async threads = 0 free

Queue = full

But HTTP request threads = still available

Those HTTP threads run the tasks in parallel, but each runs only its own task.

🎯 SUPER SIMPLE SUMMARY
✔ Requests 1–20 → async threads
✔ Requests 21–120 → async queue
✔ Requests 121–500 → caller threads

(but caller threads = one per HTTP request)

❌ They do NOT run all at once in one big batch
✔ They run one by one per incoming request thread